---
import LookoutPage from '../../components/LookoutPage.astro'
import { pages, site } from '../../site'
---

<LookoutPage
  page={pages.lookoutTerms}
  label="Terms"
  title="Lookout terms. WCGW"
  description="The terms for using Lookout, WCGW's Mac app."
>
  <h1 id="notice">Lookout terms of service</h1>
  <p>
    These terms are between you and WCGW, the studio of Radu Ceucă in Bucharest, Romania. Using
    Lookout means you accept them.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <p>
    Lookout is a Mac app that shows stats from accounts and sites you connect. It is free, and it
    reads; it never posts for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Your accounts</h2>
  <p>
    Connect only accounts you own or may use. Each service's own terms still apply, including
    <a class="link" href="https://www.tiktok.com/legal/page/global/terms-of-service/en" rel="noopener">TikTok's</a>.
    You can disconnect any account at any time. How Lookout treats your data is in the
    <a class="link" href="/lookout/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Don't</h2>
  <p>
    Use Lookout to break the law or another service's terms, or to read accounts that are not
    yours.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    Lookout is provided as is. Numbers come from other services and can be late, partial or wrong.
    A service can change or close its API, and the matching part of Lookout can stop working.
  </p>

  <h2>Liability</h2>
  <p>
    As far as the law allows, WCGW is not liable for indirect or lost-profit damages from using
    Lookout. Nothing here limits rights you have under consumer law.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes and ending</h2>
  <p>
    WCGW may change these terms; a change changes the date below. You can stop at any time by
    deleting the app. Romanian law applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p><a class="link" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></p>
</LookoutPage>
